From: mscharged-port
Subject: Present the picture at its exact shape, or stretched
Upstream-base: d664382f57002fbff143911c81b06b7e7a4b3ab4
Classification: host presentation (desktop output only). Apply after 0116.

The clean and sharp pictures showed the game's VI picture at its share of the
16:9 or 4:3 TV frame: Charged's 680x448 of 720x480 is 1.80:1 in 16:9, so a
16:9 window kept thin bars above and below it (a 960x534 picture in 960x540).
These pictures now take exactly 16:9 or 4:3, within 2 % of their own shape,
and the window-sized EFB takes the same viewport, still one EFB pixel per
window pixel. The soft picture shows the game's picture alone the same way,
as a TV hides the blank border around it in its overscan; tv_frame keeps the
whole TV frame with that border, as before.

stretch fills the whole window instead, for screens of another shape;
aurora_set_video_output_stretch switches it while the game runs, and a
window-sized EFB then takes the window's whole shape (set_frame_buffer_fill).
The presented geometry describes the stretched or cropped picture, so pointer
mapping follows it. The physical VI signal, its field history and the game's
own coordinates are unchanged.

